{
  "dom_id": "step-e10003",
  "name": "Example: Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/99a320e07046168bdd70d8168abcc6399de7e34044d6550a4d204a332110049b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* $f'(x) = 0?$",
  "worked_solution": "The derivative $f'(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/d980345045691061434f86fb96138d0d4d20a38065e6606073974cd3b8f1d7bb.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=d,$ the graph is concave up ($\\smile$) at $x=b$ only.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
